\pard\f2\b\fs28\fi-1020\li1200\fc0\cf0 Class Description\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\f0\b0\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 An NSPrinter object describes a printer's capabilities, such as whether the printer can print in color and whether it provides a particular font. An NSPrinter object represents either a particular make or type of printer, or an actual printer available to the computer. You use NSPrinter to get information about printers, not modify printer attributes or control a printing job.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 There are two ways to create an NSPrinter:\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx1800\tx6300\tx6580\tx15140\fs28\fi-300\li1800\fc0\cf0 ·	To create an abstract object that provides information about a type of printer rather than an object that represents an actual printer device, use the
\b  printerWithType:
\b0  class method, passing a printer type (an NSString) as the argument. The 
\b printerTypes
\b0  class method provides a list of printer types,  model names recognized by the computer. Printer types are described in files written in PostScript Printer Description (PPD) format. The location of these files is platform dependent.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 ·	Use the 
\b printerWithName:
\b0  class method to create or find an NSPrinter that corresponds to an actual printer device. Use the 
\b printerNames
\b0  class method to get a list of recognized printer names.\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 Once you have an NSPrinter, there's only one thing you can do with it: retrieve information regarding the type of printer or regarding the actual printer the object represents.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 When you create an NSPrinter object, the object reads the file that corresponds to the type of printer, a model name, you specified and stores the data it finds there in named tables. Printer types are described in files written in the PostScript Printer Description (PPD) format. Any piece of information in the PPD tables can be retrieved through the methods 
\b stringForKey:inTable:
\b0  and 
\b stringListForKey:inTable:
\b0 , as explained later. Commonly needed items, such as whether a printer supports color or the size of the page on which it prints, are available through more direct methods (methods such as 
\b isColor
\b0  and 
\b pageSizeForPaper:
\b0 ). \

\fs14 \

\pard\tx2300\f2\b\fs24\fi-800\li2300\fc0\cf0 Note:	
\f0\b0\fs28 To understand what the NSPrinter tables contain, you need to be acquainted with the PPD file format. This is described in 
\i PostScript Printer Description File Format Specification, version 4.0
\i0 , available from Adobe Systems Incorporated. The rest of this class description assumes a familiarity with the concepts and terminology presented in the Adobe manual. A brief summary of the PPD format is given below; PPD terms defined in the Adobe manual are shown in italic.\

\fs40 \

\pard\f2\b\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 PPD Format\

\fs8 \

\fs6 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\f0\b0\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 A PPD file statement, or 
\i entry
\i0 , associates a value with a main keyword:\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx2320\tx3740\tx4140\fs28\li2320\fc0\cf0 *mainKeyword: value\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 The asterisk is literal; it indicates the beginning of a new entry.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 For example:\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx2400\tx2880\tx3420\tx3940\tx4440\tx4920\tx7000\tx7500\tx8000\tx8500\tx9020\tx9520\tx10020\tx10520\tx11020\tx11500\f3\fs24\li1900\fc0\cf0 *ModelName: "MMimeo Machine" \
*3dDevice: False\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\f0\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 A main keyword can be qualified by an 
\i option keyword
\i0 :\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx2320\tx3740\tx4140\fs28\li2320\fc0\cf0 *mainKeyword optionKeyword: value\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 For example:\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx2400\tx2880\tx3420\tx3940\tx4440\tx4920\tx7000\tx7500\tx8000\tx8500\tx9020\tx9520\tx10020\tx10520\tx11020\tx11500\f3\fs24\li1900\fc0\cf0 *PaperDensity Letter: "0.1"\
*PaperDensity Legal: "0.2"\
*PaperDensity A4: "0.3"\
*PaperDensity B5: "0.4"\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\f0\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 In addition, any number of entries may have the same main keyword with no option keyword yet give different values:\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx2400\tx2880\tx3420\tx3940\tx4440\tx4920\tx7000\tx7500\tx8000\tx8500\tx9020\tx9520\tx10020\tx10520\tx11020\tx11500\f3\fs24\li1900\fc0\cf0 *InkName: ProcessBlack/Process Black\
*InkName: CustomColor/Custom Color\
*InkName: ProcessCyan/Process Cyan\
*InkName: ProcessMagenta/Process Magenta\
*InkName: ProcessYellow/Process Yellow\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\f0\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 Option keywords and values can sport 
\i translation strings
\i0 . A translation string is a textual description, appropriate for display in a user interface, of the option or value. An option or value is separated from its translation string by a slash:\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx2400\tx2880\tx3420\tx3940\tx4440\tx4920\tx7000\tx7500\tx8000\tx8500\tx9020\tx9520\tx10020\tx10520\tx11020\tx11500\f3\fs24\li1900\fc0\cf0 *Resolution 300dpi/300 dpi: " ... "\
*InkName: ProcessBlack/Process Black\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\f0\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 In the first example, the 
\b 300dpi
\b0  option would be presented in a user interface as ª300 dpi.º In the second example, the translation string for the 
\b ProcessBlack
\b0  value is set to ªProcess Blackº.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 NSPrinter treats entries that have an 
\b *OrderDependency
\b0  or *
\b UIConstraint
\b0  main keyword specially. Such entries take the following forms (the bracketed elements are optional):\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx2320\tx3740\tx4140\fs28\li2320\fc0\cf0 *OrderDependency: 
\i real section mainKeyword [optionKeyword] \

\fs14 \

\i0\fs28 *UIConstraint: 
\i mainKeyword1 [optionKeyword1] mainKeyword2 [optionKeyword2]\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\i0\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 There may be more than one UIConstraint entry with the same
\i  mainKeyword1
\i0  or 
\i mainKeyword1/optionKeyword1
\i0  value. Below are some examples of 
\b *OrderDependency
\b0  and 
\b *UIConstraint
\b0  entries:\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx2400\tx2880\tx3420\tx3940\tx4440\tx4920\tx7000\tx7500\tx8000\tx8500\tx9020\tx9520\tx10020\tx10520\tx11020\tx11500\f3\fs24\li1900\fc0\cf0 *OrderDependency: 10 AnySetup *Resolution\
*UIConstraint: *Option3 None *PageSize Legal\
*UIConstraint: *Option3 None *PageRegion Legal\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\f0\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 Explaining these entries is beyond the scope of this documentation; however, it's important to note their forms in order to understand how they're represented in the NSPrinter tables.   \

\fs14 \

\fs26 \

\pard\f2\b\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 NSPrinter Tables\

\fs8 \

\fs6 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\f0\b0\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 NSPrinter defines five key-value tables to store PPD information. The tables are identified by the names given below:\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx6740\tx9540\f2\b\fs28\li1900\fc0\cf0 Name	Contents\

\fs6 \

\pard\tx6740\tx9520\f0\b0\fs28\fi-4840\li6740\fc0\cf0 PPD	General information about a printer type. This table contains the values for all entries in a PPD file except those with the 
\b *OrderDependency 
\b0 and 
\b *UIConstraint
\b0  main keywords. The values in this table don't include the translation strings.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 PPDOptionTranslation	Option keyword translation strings.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 PPDArgumentTranslation	Value translation strings.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 PPDOrderDependency	
\b *OrderDependency
\b0  values.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 PPDUIConstraints	
\b *UIConstraint
\b0  values.\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 There are two principle methods for retrieving data from the NSPrinter tables:\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx1800\tx3360\tx3740\fs28\fi-300\li1800\fc0\cf0 ·	
\b stringForKey:inTable:
\b0  returns the value for the first occurrence of a given key in the given table.\
·	
\b stringListForKey:inTable:
\b0  returns an array of values, one for each occurrence of the key.\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 For both methods, the first argument is an NSString that names a keyÐwhich part of a PPD file entry the key corresponds to depends on the table (as explained in the following sections). The second argument names the table that you want to search. The values that are returned by these methods, whether singular or in an array, are always NSStrings, even if the value wasn't a quoted string in the PPD file.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 The NSPrinter tables store data as ASCII text, thus the two methods described above are sufficient for retrieving any value from any table. NSPrinter provides a number of other methods, such as 
\b booleanForKey:inTable:
\b0  and 
\b intForKey:inTable:
\b0 , that retrieve single values and coerce them, if possible, into particular data types. The coercion doesn't affect the data that's stored in the table (it remains in ASCII format).\

\fs14 \

\fs28 To check the integrity of a table, use the 
\b isKey:forTable:
\b0  and 
\b statusForTable:
\b0  methods. The former returns a boolean that indicates whether the given key is valid for the given table; the latter returns an error code that describes the general state of a table (in particular, whether it actually exists). \

\fs14 \

\fs26 \

\pard\f2\b\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 Retrieving Values from the PPD Table\

\fs8 \

\fs6 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\f0\b0\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 Keys for the PPD table are strings that name a main keyword or main keyword/option keyword pairing (formatted as 
\i ªmainKeyword/optionKeywordº
\i0 ). In both cases, you exclude the main keyword asterisk. The following example creates an NSPrinter and invokes 
\b stringForKey:inTable:
\b0  to retrieve the value for an un-optioned main keyword:\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx2400\tx2880\tx3420\tx3940\tx4440\tx4920\tx7000\tx7500\tx8000\tx8500\tx9020\tx9520\tx10020\tx10520\tx11020\tx11500\f3\fs24\li1900\fc0\cf0 /* Create an NSPrinter object for a printer type. */\
NSPrinter *prType = [NSPrinter printerWithType:@"My_Mimeo_Machine"]\
\
/* Sets sValue to FALSE. */\
NSString *sValue = [prType stringForKey:@"3dDevice" inTable:@"PPD"];\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\f0\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 To retrieve the value for a main keyword/option keyword pair, pass the keywords formatted as 
\i ªmainKeyword/optionKeywordº
\i0 :\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx2400\tx2880\tx3420\tx3940\tx4440\tx4920\tx7000\tx7500\tx8000\tx8500\tx9020\tx9520\tx10020\tx10520\tx11020\tx11500\f3\fs24\li1900\fc0\cf0 /* Sets sValue to "0.3". */\
NSString *sValue = [prType stringForKey:@"PaperDensity/A4" inTable:@"PPD"];\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\f0\b\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 stringForKey:inTable:
\b0  can determine if a main keyword has options. If you pass a main keyword (only) as the first argument to the method, and if that keyword has options in the PPD file, the method returns an empty string. If it doesn't have options, it returns the value of the first occurrence of the main keyword:\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx2400\tx2880\tx3420\tx3940\tx4440\tx4920\tx7000\tx7500\tx8000\tx8500\tx9020\tx9520\tx10020\tx10520\tx11020\tx11500\f3\fs24\li1900\fc0\cf0 /* Sets sValue to an empty string. */\
NSString *sValue = [prType stringForKey:@"PaperDensity" inTable:@"PPD"];\
\
/* Sets sValue to "ProcessBlack". */\
NSString *sValue = [prType stringForKey:@"InkName" inTable:@"PPD"];\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\f0\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 To retrieve the values for all occurrences of a main keyword, use the 
\b stringListForKey:inTable:
\b0  method giving the main keyword only:\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx2400\tx2880\tx3420\tx3940\tx4440\tx4920\tx7000\tx7500\tx8000\tx8500\tx9020\tx9520\tx10020\tx10520\tx11020\tx11500\f3\fs24\li1900\fc0\cf0 /* Sets sList to an array containing "ProcessBlack","CustomColor", etc. */\
NSArray *sList = [prType stringListForKey:@"InkName" inTable:@"PPD"];\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\f0\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 In addition, 
\b stringListForKey:inTable:
\b0  can be used to retrieve all the options for a main keyword (given that the main keyword has options):\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx2400\tx2880\tx3420\tx3940\tx4440\tx4920\tx7000\tx7500\tx8000\tx8500\tx9020\tx9520\tx10020\tx10520\tx11020\tx11500\f3\fs24\li1900\fc0\cf0 /* Sets sList to an array containing "Letter", "Legal","A4", etc. */\
NSArray *sList = [prType stringListForKey:@"PaperDensity" inTable:@"PPD"];\

\fs40 \

\pard\f2\b\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 Retrieving Values from the Option and Argument Translation Tables\

\fs8 \

\fs6 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\f0\b0\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 A key to a translation table is similiar to a key  to the PPD table: It's a main keyword or main/option keyword pair (again excluding the asterisk). However, the values that are returned from the translation tables are the translation strings for the option or argument (value) portions of the PPD file entry. For example:\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx2400\tx2880\tx3420\tx3940\tx4440\tx4920\tx7000\tx7500\tx8000\tx8500\tx9020\tx9520\tx10020\tx10520\tx11020\tx11500\f3\fs24\li1900\fc0\cf0 /* Sets sValue to "300 dpi". */\
NSString *sValue = [prType stringForKey:@"Resolution/300dpi" \
                                inTable:@"PPDOptionTranslation"];\
\
/* Sets sList to an array containing "Process Black", "Custom Color", etc. */\
NSArray *sList = [prType stringListForKey:@"InkName"\
                                  inTable:@"PPDArgumentTranslation"];\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\f0\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 As with the PPD table, use 
\b stringListForKey:inTable:
\b0  to request an array of all occurances of a main keyword.\

\fs14 \

\fs26 \

\pard\f2\b\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 Retrieving Values from the Order Dependency Table\

\fs8 \

\fs6 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\f0\b0\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 As mentioned earlier, an order dependency entry takes this form:\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx2320\tx3740\tx4140\fs28\li2320\fc0\cf0 *OrderDependency: 
\i real section mainKeyword [optionKeyword] \

\fs14 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\i0\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 These entries are stored in the PPDOrderDependency table. To retrieve a value from this table, always use 
\b stringListForKey:inTable:
\b0 . The value passed as the key is, again, a main keyword or main keyword/option keyword pair; however, these values correspond to the 
\i mainKeyword
\i0  and 
\i optionKeyword
\i0  parts of an order dependency entry's value. As with the other tables, the main keyword's asterisk is excluded. The method returns an NSArray of two NSStrings that correspond to the 
\i real
\i0  and 
\i section
\i0  values for the entry. For example:\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx2400\tx2880\tx3420\tx3940\tx4440\tx4920\tx7000\tx7500\tx8000\tx8500\tx9020\tx9520\tx10020\tx10520\tx11020\tx11500\f3\fs24\li1900\fc0\cf0 /* Sets sList to an array containing "10" and "AnySetup". */\
NSArray *sList = [prType stringListForKey:@"Resolution"\
                                  inTable:@"PPDOrderDependency"]\

\fs40 \

\pard\f2\b\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 Retrieving Values from the UIConstraints Table\

\fs8 \

\fs6 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\f0\b0\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 Retrieving a value from the PPDUIConstraints table is similar to retrieving a value from the PPDOrderDependency table: always use 
\b stringListForKey:inTable:
\b0  and the key corresponds to elements in the entry's value. Given the following form (as described earlier), the key corresponds to 
\i mainKeyword1/optionKeyword1
\i0 :\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx2320\tx3740\tx4140\fs28\li2320\fc0\cf0 *UIConstraint: 
\i mainKeyword1
\i0  [
\i optionKeyword1
\i0 ] 
\i mainKeyword2
\i0  [
\i optionKeyword2
\i0 ]\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 The NSArray that's returned by 
\b stringListForKey:inTable:
\b0  contains the 
\i mainKeyword2
\i0  and 
\i optionKeyword2
\i0  values (with the keywords stored as separate elements in the NSArray) for every 
\b *UIConstraints
\b0  entry that has the given 
\i mainKeyword1/optionKeyword1
\i0  value. For example:\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx2400\tx2880\tx3420\tx3940\tx4440\tx4920\tx7000\tx7500\tx8000\tx8500\tx9020\tx9520\tx10020\tx10520\tx11020\tx11500\f3\fs24\li1900\fc0\cf0 /* Sets sList to an array containing:\
   "PageSize", "Legal", "PageRegion", and "Legal" */\
NSArray *sList = [prType stringListForKey:@"Option3/None" \
                                  inTable:@"PPDUIConstraints"]\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\f0\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 Note that the main keywords that are returned in the NSArray don't have asterisks. Also, the NSArray that's returned always alternates main and option keywords. If a particular main keyword doesn't have an option associated with it, the string for the option will be empty (but the entry in the NSArray for the option 
\i will
\i0  exist).\

\fs14 \

